{
  "scenarioVersion": 3,
  "title": "The Quiet Ledger",
  "description": "A bookkeeper hired to audit a merchant house's accounts finds its new head reading every contract twice and hoping nobody notices how lost he is. The contract renewals are three weeks away.",
  "prompt": "The rain had followed me all the way up the hill, and it was still dripping off my satchel strap when Hollis Bray opened the side door of House Varlen.\n\"Miss Fenlow. You found us.\" Hollis stepped back to let me in and took my wet coat before I could argue. \"I did say the front steps were slippery. Nobody listens to the steward.\"\n\"I listened,\" I said, and wiped my boots twice on the mat to prove it. \"I just came the long way so I could look at your warehouses first.\"\nHe laughed at that and led me down a corridor of good rugs laid over cold stone. A portrait gallery opened on the left. One hook near the end held no painting, and I counted the frames without meaning to.\n\"There was a winter, oh, forty years back,\" Hollis said, and slowed so I could keep pace, \"when the old master kept the ledgers in the pantry because it was the only room with a lock that worked. We found the spring accounts under a sack of onions.\"\n\"Were they right?\" I said, and shoved a slipping pin back into my hair.\nHollis stopped at a door with a brass plate that read COUNTING ROOM. He kept his hand on the handle a moment longer than he needed to. \"They were right,\" he said. \"I'd like to be able to say the same this year.\"\nHe knocked once and went in. A tall man sat at the desk with a contract held close to his face, and his lips moved as he read. A good coat hung off his shoulders and stopped short of his wrists. He looked up, saw me, and stood so fast that his chair rocked back against the shelves.\n\"Hollis,\" the man said, and set the contract down face first. \"Honestly, you could warn a person. Look, if this is about the window tax, I've already paid it. Twice, I think.\"\n\"This is Miss Mira Fenlow, young sir.\" Hollis folded his hands behind his back. \"A bookkeeper. I've engaged her to go through the house accounts before the council sits.\"\nJosselin Varlen looked from Hollis to me and back. The tips of his ears went red. He tugged at one cuff, then the other. \"You've engaged her,\" he said. \"To audit my books. Without mentioning it to the person whose books they are.\"\n\"Right,\" I said, and set my satchel on the corner of his desk because nobody had offered me a chair. \"Would you like me to come back once you've both decided whether I work here?\"\nJosselin opened his mouth and closed it again. Then he laughed and pulled the second chair out from the wall with his foot. \"No. Sit. Please. I've been reading that contract since breakfast, and I'm fairly sure it's reading me back.\"\nHollis was already halfway to the door. \"I'll bring tea,\" he said, and the latch clicked shut behind him.\nThe counting room smelled of dust and lamp oil and wet wool. Josselin pushed the contract across the desk toward me with two fingers and waited for me to pick it up.",
  "tags": [
    "cozy fantasy",
    "slow-burn romance",
    "light political intrigue"
  ],
